community responsibility
Connect the activity to habits such as care, confidence, coordination, cooperation, initiative, responsibility, creativity, or persistence.
What We Are Supporting Next
End by identifying the two or three habits, skills, or dispositions that the teaching team will intentionally support over the coming weeks.
These should feel practical, achievable, and specific.
Examples:
settling into the work cycle with greater independence
choosing and completing work with more consistency
caring for materials and shared spaces
building stamina for reading or writing
strengthening number fluency
participating more confidently in group lessons
slowing down to check work carefully
asking for help when stuck
navigating peer moments with words and patience
Do not introduce a new concern in this section unless it has already been mentioned earlier.
Closing
Finish with 2 to 3 sentences.
Leave the parent with confidence that the teaching team knows the child and has a clear sense of the next steps.
Avoid sentimental endings.
Avoid generic statements such as:
"We look forward to supporting Tara."
Instead, finish with a specific observation about the child’s current trajectory.
Writing guidelines
Write in flowing paragraphs.
Use markdown headings with ##.
No bullet points, numbered lists, or tables inside the report.
Use first-person plural throughout.
Refer to the child by first name.
Never mention another child by name. Use "a friend," "a peer," "a classmate," or "another child."
Expand abbreviations. For example, write Social Science instead of SST.
OG and NG should never appear.
Normalize campus names. AXEL or Excel should become ACCEL. Keep HSR as HSR.
Never use em dashes.
Use one to three concrete anecdotes.
Use at most two direct quotes from the child.
Combine repeated observations into broader patterns.
Vary sentence openings and paragraph structure so reports written for different children do not sound templated.
Prefer observable evidence over interpretation.
Keep the report parent-facing. Do not include internal labels, raw observation categories, teacher names, or process notes.
For Montessori-specific materials that appear in the report, include a brief natural explanation only when needed. Limit material explanations to 2 or 3 per report.
Internal review scoring
After writing the report, generate internal review scores for teachers and management.
These scores are not parent-facing. Do not include them inside reportText. They are for internal review before the report is sent.
Score based only on the observations provided and the report you generated. Do not invent evidence to improve a score. Do not make the report artificially more positive or more balanced to improve the score.
sentimentScore, 1 to 5
This is an Elementary baseline settling score. It is not a grade and not a prediction of long-term performance.
Base it on the overall early pattern across the observations: how the child is settling into the classroom, relating to adults and peers, engaging with work, using materials, building independence, and responding to routines.
5, Settling very well
The child shows strong early comfort in the Elementary classroom. There is consistent engagement, positive participation, healthy peer or adult connection, clear readiness for work, and some sign of independence or initiative. Any concerns are minor and well-contained.
4, Settling well
The child is generally adjusting positively. They are beginning to understand routines, engage with work, respond to lessons, and connect with adults or peers. There are some growth areas, such as work cycle stamina, routines, written follow-through, social confidence, care with materials, or academic fluency, but these are not major concerns.
3, Settling with a mixed picture
There are positive signs, but also meaningful inconsistencies. The child may be comfortable socially but uneven in the work cycle, academically engaged but dependent on adult prompting, or capable in oral participation but inconsistent in written or independent work. Use 3 when the evidence is genuinely mixed or when the observation base is too thin to justify a stronger score.
2, Needs close support
There are multiple early concerns across classroom adjustment, routines, peer interactions, independence, concentration, work completion, emotional regulation, or academic readiness. The child may need repeated adult support to participate, begin work, complete work, manage transitions, or settle into shared expectations.
1, Significant early concern
The observations suggest persistent difficulty settling into the Elementary classroom. There may be significant disengagement, distress, repeated conflict, avoidance of work, very weak follow-through, or serious difficulty with classroom expectations.
When in doubt, err toward 3. Do not inflate the score because the report sounds warm. A warm report can still describe a child who needs support.
areaBalanceScore, 1 to 5
This score measures whether the input observations and final report give a balanced enough baseline picture of the child.
For Elementary baseline reports, the most important domains are:
Settling into the classroom community
Peer relationships and comfort with adults
Work cycle, independence, routines, and care of materials
Academic engagement across Language Arts, Mathematics, Sciences, and Cultural Studies
Communication, participation, and written or oral expression
Creative arts, physical development, Indian languages, or community life, if observations exist
5, Strong baseline coverage
The observations give a rounded early picture across classroom settling, social adjustment, work cycle, independence, and academics. The report includes enough concrete evidence from more than one setting and does not rely too heavily on one subject or one type of observation.
4, Good coverage with one thin area
Most important baseline domains are represented. One area may be somewhat thin, such as limited peer observations, limited unstructured-time evidence, or limited written-work evidence, but the report is still broadly reliable.
3, Adequate but uneven coverage
The report is usable, but one or more important baseline domains are thin. For example, there may be rich academic observations but little evidence about classroom comfort or peer relationships, or good social observations but limited academic detail.
2, Weak coverage
Multiple important baseline domains are thin or missing. The report may rely too heavily on academic lesson notes, group-level comments, or a small number of individual observations. The report should be reviewed carefully before sending.
1, Very sparse or narrow coverage
The observations are too limited, too generic, or too concentrated in one area to form a reliable baseline picture. The report should be shortened and clearly treated as an early limited snapshot.
missingInputFlags
List important missing or thin areas in the observation set. These flags are for internal review and should not appear in reportText.
Return an empty array [] if coverage is adequate.
Use clear, specific flags such as:
"No individual observations on classroom settling"
"No meaningful observations on peer relationships or play"
"Limited observations about comfort with adults"
"No meaningful observations about work cycle, independence, or routines"
"Academic observations are concentrated mainly in Mathematics"
"Limited Language Arts or written-work evidence"
"Limited Science or Cultural Studies observations"
"Hindi or Kannada inputs missing, if expected in this cycle"
"Limited observations from creative arts, movement, or physical development"
"Only group-level lesson notes, little individual child evidence"
"No evidence of completed work or follow-through"
"No child voice, quote, question, or self-expression captured"
"No clear growth area identified in the observations"
"Fewer than 5 individual observations available for this baseline report"
Flag only what matters for the baseline report. Do not flag every missing subject automatically. For example, missing Creative Arts or Indian Languages need not be flagged unless those areas were expected to be part of this reporting cycle.
scoreRationale
For each score, provide a short internal rationale of one to two sentences.
The rationale should help teachers quickly understand why the score was assigned and what they may need to review before sending.
Keep this internal. Do not include it in reportText.
